'''Nicolas Hülkenberg''' ({{IPA|de|ˈniːko ˈhʏlkənbɛɐ̯k}}; born 19 August 1987) is a German [[racing driver]] who competes in [[Formula One]] for [[Audi in Formula One|Audi]]. In [[endurance racing (motorsport)|endurance racing]], Hülkenberg won the [[24 Hours of Le Mans]] in {{24hLM|2015}} with [[Porsche in motorsport|Porsche]].

Born and raised in [[Emmerich am Rhein]], Hülkenberg began competitive [[kart racing]] at age 10, winning several national titles before graduating to [[junior formulae]] in 2005. Hülkenberg won his first championship in [[2005 Formula BMW ADAC season|Formula BMW ADAC]] that year. He then won the [[2006–07 A1 Grand Prix season|2006–07 A1 Grand Prix]] series, representing [[A1 Team Germany|Germany]]. After winning the [[2007 Masters of Formula 3]] and the [[2008 Formula 3 Euro Series]], Hülkenberg progressed to the [[GP2 Series]] in [[2009 GP2 Series|2009]] with [[ART Grand Prix|ART]]; he won the championship in his debut season, becoming the third driver to win the GP2/[[FIA Formula 2 Championship|Formula 2]] title in their rookie season after [[Nico Rosberg]] and [[Lewis Hamilton]].

Following test driver roles from {{F1|2007}} to {{F1|2009}}, Hülkenberg signed for [[Williams Racing|Williams]] in {{F1|2010}}, making his Formula One debut at the {{F1GP|2010|Bahrain}}. After claiming his maiden [[List of Formula One polesitters|pole position]] at the {{F1GP|2010|Brazilian}}, he was replaced by [[Pastor Maldonado]] at the end of the season. He joined [[Force India]] as a reserve driver in {{F1|2011}}. He was then promoted to a full-time seat for the {{F1|2012}} season. In {{F1|2013}}, he competed for [[Sauber]], before returning to Force India in {{F1|2014}} to partner [[Sergio Pérez]]. After his {{F1|2015}} and {{F1|2016}} campaigns at Force India, Hülkenberg switched to [[Renault in Formula One|Renault]] for {{F1|2017}}. Replaced by [[Esteban Ocon]] at the end of {{F1|2019}}, he returned to Force India—then known as [[Racing Point]] and later re-branded as [[Aston Martin in Formula One|Aston Martin]]—as a reserve driver from {{F1|2020}} to {{F1|2022}}; Hülkenberg substituted for Pérez and [[Lance Stroll]] at three Grands Prix in 2020, and for [[Sebastian Vettel]] at two in 2022. He returned as a full-time driver with [[Haas F1 Team|Haas]] in {{F1|2023}} and {{F1|2024}}, alongside [[Kevin Magnussen]]. Hülkenberg re-joined Sauber for his {{F1|2025}} campaign, where he achieved his maiden podium at the {{F1GP|2025|British}}, ahead of their acquisition by [[Audi in Formula One|Audi]] in {{F1|2026}}.

As of the {{Latest F1GP}}, Hülkenberg has achieved {{Numbertoword|{{f1stat|HUL|poles}}}} pole position, {{Numbertoword|{{f1stat|HUL|fastestlaps}}}} fastest laps, and {{Numbertoword|{{f1stat|HUL|podiums}}}} podium finish in Formula One. He holds the [[List of Formula One driver records|Formula One records]] for the [[List of Formula One driver records#Most races without a win|most starts without a win]] ({{f1stat|HUL|starts}}) and the [[List of Formula One driver records#Most races before scoring a podium finish|most starts before a podium]] (239), before breaking the podium-less streak at the [[2025 British Grand Prix]] after finishing in 3rd place, behind race winner [[Lando Norris]] and his [[McLaren]] teammate [[Oscar Piastri]] in 2nd. Hülkenberg is contracted to remain at Audi until at least the end of the 2026 season.

== Early and personal life ==
Hülkenberg was born in [[Emmerich am Rhein]], North Rhine-Westphalia, [[West Germany]] to Klaus Dieter Hülkenberg and Susanne Hülkenberg. Dieter Hülkenberg owns a shipping company, Hülkenberg Spedition e. K, based in Emmerich am Rhein. Hülkenberg trained as a freight forwarding agent at his father's company.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.speedweek.com/steckbrief/301/Nico-Huelkenberg.html|title=SPEEDWEEK Steckbrief Nico Hülkenberg|website=Speedweek.com|language=de-DE|access-date=29 April 2017|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162655/https://www.speedweek.com/steckbrief/301/Nico-Huelkenberg.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He is fluent in German, Dutch, French, and English.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pitpass.com/29440/Hulkenberg-to-open-for-Germany|title=Hulkenberg to open for Germany|website=Pitpass.com|date=28 September 2006|access-date=29 April 2017|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162654/https://www.pitpass.com/29440/Hulkenberg-to-open-for-Germany|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg lives in [[Monaco]]. He is married to Lithuanian fashion designer Eglė Ruškytė, having been in a relationship with her since 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 June 2021 |title=Who is Nico Hulkenberg's Wife – Egle Ruskyte? |url=https://www.essentiallysports.com/f1-news-who-is-nico-hulkenbergs-wife-egle-ruskyte/ |access-date=24 May 2022 |website=Essentially Sports |language=en-gb |archive-date=6 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221106171804/https://www.essentiallysports.com/f1-news-who-is-nico-hulkenbergs-wife-egle-ruskyte/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Together, they have one daughter born in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 September 2021 |title=Congratulations Pour in From Up and Down the F1 Paddock as Nico Hulkenberg Welcomes Baby Daughter |url=https://www.essentiallysports.com/f1-news-congratulations-pour-in-from-up-and-down-the-f1-paddock-as-nico-hulkenberg-welcomes-baby-daughter/ |access-date=24 May 2022 |website=Essentially Sports |language=en-gb |archive-date=23 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220523223248/https://www.essentiallysports.com/f1-news-congratulations-pour-in-from-up-and-down-the-f1-paddock-as-nico-hulkenberg-welcomes-baby-daughter/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Hülkenberg was previously managed by [[Willi Weber]], the long-time manager of [[Michael Schumacher]]. Weber predicted that Hülkenberg would be ready for [[Formula One]] by {{f1|2008}}. He also praised Hülkenberg as an "unbelievable talent" and said he reminded him of Schumacher as a young driver. He also stated that he nicknamed him "[[Hulk|The Hulk]]", after the fictional superhero, in reference to Hülkenberg changing his personality whilst at the wheel.<ref name = "hulkenberg-weber">{{cite web | title=Weber: he's the next Schumi! | work=Overdrive | date=5 December 2006 | url=http://www.odmotoring.com/index.php?display=racing&id=125 | access-date=5 December 2006 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927194823/http://www.odmotoring.com/index.php?display=racing&id=125 | archive-date=27 September 2007 | url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Junior racing career==
[[File:Hulkenberg Sepang 2006.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg, as a driver for [[A1 Team Germany]], in 2006]]
===Karting===
Hülkenberg made his [[kart racing|karting]] debut in 1997, at the age of 10. In 2002 he was German Junior Karting Champion and the following year he won the German Kart Championship.<ref name="meethulkenberg">{{cite web | title = Meet the rookies: Nico Hülkenberg | publisher = F1Fanatic.co.uk | year = 2007 | url = http://www.f1fanatic.co.uk/2007/08/16/meet-the-rookies-nico-hulkenberg/ | access-date = 16 August 2007 | archive-date = 19 September 2011 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110919210331/http://www.f1fanatic.co.uk/2007/08/16/meet-the-rookies-nico-hulkenberg/ | url-status = live }}</ref>

===Formula BMW===
Hülkenberg made his German [[Formula BMW]] debut in 2005, dominating the championship and winning the title comfortably. He finished first in the Formula BMW world final but was stripped of the win after it was claimed he had brake-tested his rivals during a safety car period.<ref name="meethulkenberg" />

===A1 Grand Prix===
Hülkenberg also joined the [[A1 Team Germany|German A1 Grand Prix team]] for the [[2006–07 A1 Grand Prix season|2006–07 season]]. Nine wins in his rookie season made him the most successful driver in A1GP history. It meant he almost single-handedly won Germany the championship with 128 points, 35 more than [[A1 Team New Zealand|Team New Zealand]].<ref name="meethulkenberg" />

===Formula Three===
[[File:Nico Hülkenberg.JPG|thumb|left|Hülkenberg won seven races en route to the [[2008 Formula 3 Euro Series]] title.]]
Hülkenberg finished fifth in the [[German Formula Three Championship]] (ATS Formel 3 Cup) in 2006. For [[2007 Formula 3 Euro Series|2007]] he switched to the [[Formula 3 Euro Series]] with the [[ART Grand Prix|ASM]] team that had taken [[Lewis Hamilton]] and [[Paul di Resta]] to the last two championships. His first win came at the [[Norisring]]<ref name="meethulkenberg" /> from 18th on the grid, he won again in the rain at [[Zandvoort]]<ref name="mmhulkzandvoort">{{cite web | title = F3 Euro Zandvoort 2: Hülkenberg wins in wet | publisher = MaximumMotorsport.co.uk | year = 2007 | url = http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/07/29/f3-euro-zandvoort-2-hulkenberg-wins-in-wet/ | access-date =16 August 2007 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20071203054933/http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/07/29/f3-euro-zandvoort-2-hulkenberg-wins-in-wet/ <!-- Bot retrieved archive --> |archive-date = 3 December 2007}}</ref> and added a third at the [[Nürburgring]].<ref name="mmhulknurburgring">{{cite web | title = F3 Euro Nürburgring 1: Hülkenberg dominates | publisher = MaximumMotorsport.co.uk | year = 2007 | url = http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/09/02/f3-euro-nurburgring-hulkenberg-dominates/ | access-date =3 September 2007 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20071103004540/http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/09/02/f3-euro-nurburgring-hulkenberg-dominates/ <!-- Bot retrieved archive --> |archive-date = 3 November 2007}}</ref> But he ran into trouble at [[Circuit de Nevers Magny-Cours|Magny-Cours]], being penalised in qualifying for passing the chequered flag twice, and crashing into [[Filip Salaquarda]] in the race.<ref name="meethulkenberg" /> Hülkenberg finished his rookie season 3rd in the championship, with four wins and 72 points.

Hülkenberg won the non-championship [[Masters of Formula 3]] race at [[Circuit Zolder|Zolder]] from teammate (and F3 Euro Series championship leader) [[Romain Grosjean]] after Grosjean stalled at the start.<ref name="mmhulkmasters">{{cite web | title = Hülkenberg wins F3 Masters as Grosjean stalls | publisher = MaximumMotorsport.co.uk | year = 2007 | url = http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/08/05/hulkenberg-wins-masters-as-grosjean-stalls/ | access-date =16 August 2007 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20071009235246/http://www.maximummotorsport.co.uk/2007/08/05/hulkenberg-wins-masters-as-grosjean-stalls/ <!-- Bot retrieved archive --> |archive-date = 9 October 2007}}</ref> Hülkenberg won the Formula 3 Euro Series championship in [[2008 Formula 3 Euro Series|2008]]. He amassed 76 of his total of 85 points during the feature races on Saturdays, taking seven wins in the progress.{{citation needed|date=June 2015}}

===GP2===
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2009 GP2 Monaco.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg driving for [[ART Grand Prix]] at the [[2009 Monaco GP2 Series round]]]]
Hülkenberg made his [[GP2 Asia Series]] début for the [[ART Grand Prix]] team at the third round of the [[2008–09 GP2 Asia Series]] in [[2009 Bahrain 1st GP2 Asia Series round|Bahrain]], replacing [[Pastor Maldonado]], and he took pole position on his first attempt.<ref>{{cite news| title =Hulkenberg takes pole on GP2 debut| work =Autosport| url =http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/72942| date =22 January 2009| access-date =22 January 2009| archive-date =24 January 2009| archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20090124205929/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/72942| url-status =live}}</ref> He finished both races in fourth place and this left him in seventh place in the championship. His second race weekend in [[2009 Qatar GP2 Asia Series round|Qatar]], saw him become the first night race pole-sitter, and promptly turned that into becoming the first race-winner under lights after a dominant performance. Such was his performance that he ended up over thirteen seconds clear of second-placed driver [[Sergio Pérez]].<ref>{{cite news| title =Hulkenberg storms to Qatar victory| work =Autosport| url =http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/73272| date =13 February 2009| access-date =13 February 2009| archive-date =8 June 2011| archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20110608191756/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/73272| url-status =live}}</ref> He finished third in the sprint race, taking his championship points tally to 27 from just four races. Despite this, Maldonado returned to the seat for the final two rounds and Hülkenberg finished sixth in the championship.
[[File:Nico Hülkenberg GP2 Germany R5 2009 002 (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg celebrates victory at the [[2009 Nürburgring GP2 Series round]]|250px]]

Hülkenberg continued with ART into the [[2009 GP2 Series]], partnering [[Pastor Maldonado]]. He was point-less in the first round in [[2009 Catalunya GP2 Series round|Catalunya]], then scored his first points in [[2009 Monaco GP2 Series round|Monaco]], including a podium in the sprint race, where the top eight on the starting grid are the feature race finishers in reversed order. At the following round at [[2009 Istanbul Park GP2 Series round|Istanbul Park]] he took his first pole position, but did not make the podium in either race. At [[2009 Silverstone GP2 Series round|Silverstone]] he again scored in both races, including a podium in the sprint race. He took his first win in dominant fashion at his [[2009 Nürburgring GP2 Series round|home round]], starting from pole position and taking the fastest lap. In the sprint race he rose from eighth to win the race with the fastest lap, becoming the first driver to do the weekend double since [[Giorgio Pantano]] at [[2006 Monza GP2 Series round|Monza]] in the [[2006 GP2 Series|2006 season]]. In doing so, he became only the second driver to complete the clean sweep, with pole position, two fastest laps and two wins, following [[Nelson Piquet Jr.]] who did so at the [[2006 Hungaroring GP2 Series round|Hungaroring]] in 2006. Hülkenberg won again at the [[2009 Hungaroring GP2 Series round|Hungaroring]], in the feature race; again at [[2009 Valencia GP2 Series round|Valencia]], this time during the sprint race, having finished second while starting from pole in the feature race; then came home second in the [[2009 Spa-Francorchamps GP2 Series round|Belgian]] feature race. He clinched the title with two races to spare, after a third-place finish in the [[2009 Monza GP2 Series round|Monza]] sprint race which gave him an unassailable 22-point lead heading into the final round, and in the process became the first driver to clinch the championship without the need for a final round decider. He became the third rookie GP2 champion after [[Nico Rosberg]] and [[Lewis Hamilton]], and the second behind Hamilton to win the GP3/F3 title, and the GP2/F2 title in consecutive years. A fifth win followed at [[2009 Algarve GP2 Series round|Algarve]], allowing Hülkenberg to break the 100-point barrier, as he eventually won the title by 25 points from [[Vitaly Petrov]]. Worthy of note is that Hülkenberg ended the season 64 points clear of his teammate [[Pastor Maldonado]], who would win the title the following season and take his Williams F1 race seat for the [[2011 Formula One World Championship|2011 season]].

==Formula One career==
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2010 Malaysia 2nd Free Practice.jpg|thumb|Hülkenberg scored his first World Championship point at the [[2010 Malaysian Grand Prix]].]]
Hülkenberg first drove a [[Formula One]] car in a test for [[Williams Grand Prix Engineering|Williams]] in 2007. His manager, [[Willi Weber]], had organised the test after failing to conclude a deal with Renault boss [[Flavio Briatore]]. The two-day test was held at the [[Circuito de Jerez]] in Spain, and Hülkenberg outpaced Williams's driver [[Kazuki Nakajima]], and set laptimes 0.4 seconds slower than [[Nico Rosberg]].<ref name="Williams sign Hulkenberg as test driver">{{cite web|title=Williams sign Hulkenberg as test driver|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/64371|first=Pablo|last=Elizalde|work=Autosport|date=13 December 2007|access-date=26 March 2012|archive-date=8 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608085146/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/64371|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg's performance at the test led to the Williams team signing him as a [[test driver]], and he competed in several test sessions in addition to racing in lower formulae.<ref name="Williams sign Hulkenberg as test driver" /> His test contract with Williams was extended for 2009, despite in-season tests being banned from that season.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.itv-f1.com/news_article.aspx?id=45164|title=Hulkenberg not perturbed by test ban|work=ITV F1|publisher=[[ITV Sport]]|date=25 February 2009|access-date=6 November 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090416073948/http://www.itv-f1.com/news_article.aspx?id=45164|archive-date=16 April 2009}}</ref> Hülkenberg would also act as the team's [[Williams Driver Academy|reserve driver]], in the event of the regular drivers being unable to compete.

=== Williams (2010) ===
On 2 November 2009, Hülkenberg was confirmed to race for Williams in {{F1|2010}}.<ref>{{cite news|title=2010 Driver Lineup|website=Williamsf1.com|url=http://www.williamsf1.com/news/view/1182|date=2 November 2009|access-date=2 November 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091105020600/http://www.williamsf1.com/news/view/1182|archive-date=5 November 2009}}</ref> Hülkenberg's teammate for his first season would be the experienced driver [[Rubens Barrichello]], who moved from [[Brawn GP]] which in turn was bought out by [[Mercedes-Benz]].

Hülkenberg made his debut at the {{F1 GP|2010|Bahrain}}, recovering from an early spin to finish in fourteenth position. At the second round in [[2010 Australian Grand Prix|Australia]], he was involved in a first-lap incident with [[Kamui Kobayashi]], after the Japanese driver's front wing failed and sent him into the barrier, rebounding into the path of Hülkenberg. At the third round in [[2010 Malaysian Grand Prix|Malaysia]], Hülkenberg made it to Q3 for the first time, qualifying in fifth place; out-qualifying teammate Barrichello for the first time. Hülkenberg looked set to finish eleventh in the race until [[Fernando Alonso]] blew his engine three laps from the end, thus promoting Hülkenberg to tenth place and with the new-for-2010 [[List of Formula One World Championship points scoring systems|points system]], Hülkenberg along with [[Jaime Alguersuari]] scored their first points in Formula One. He was tenth again at Silverstone, and at the {{F1 GP|2010|Hungarian}} he finished sixth, a career best. He also picked up points finishes in Italy, Singapore, and Korea. At the {{F1 GP|2010|Japanese}}, [[Renault in Formula One|Renault]] driver Vitaly Petrov misjudged a move at the start and cut across Hülkenberg's nose thus taking them both out of the race. Towards the end of the season there were reports that he could lose his seat at Williams to the [[2010 GP2 Series|GP2 Series]] champion [[Pastor Maldonado]] due to the money Maldonado could bring to the team.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://esporte.ig.com.br/grandepremio/formula1/2010/10/19/com+r+35+milhoes+maldonado+assina+com+williams+para+2011+revela+revista+warm+up+9749098.html|language=pt|work=Grande Prêmio|publisher=Internet Group|date=19 October 2010|access-date=6 November 2010|title=Maldonado assina com Williams para 2011, garante Revista Warm Up|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101022143338/http://esporte.ig.com.br/grandepremio/formula1/2010/10/19/com+r+35+milhoes+maldonado+assina+com+williams+para+2011+revela+revista+warm+up+9749098.html|archive-date=22 October 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Force India]]'s [[Adrian Sutil]] was also linked to replace Hülkenberg at Williams.

On 6 November, Hülkenberg gained his first Formula One pole position, by 1.049 seconds over [[Sebastian Vettel]] at the {{F1 GP|2010|Brazilian}} in a rain-affected qualifying session. This was the Williams team's first pole position since the [[2005 European Grand Prix]]. Hülkenberg completed a final lap after pole position was already secured, increasing his gap to the rest of the field.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/motorsport/formula_one/9163670.stm|first=Andrew|last=Benson|publisher=BBC Sport|location=Autódromo José Carlos Pace, São Paulo|date=6 November 2010|access-date=6 November 2010|title=Nico Hulkenberg takes Brazilian GP pole for Williams|archive-date=15 December 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191215081348/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/motorsport/formula_one/9163670.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> After losing the lead on the opening lap, he eventually finished the race in eighth place, having been passed by drivers in more competitive cars.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2010/nov/07/brazilian-grand-prix-sebastian-vettel|date=7 November 2010|access-date=27 March 2024|archive-date=25 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190925072642/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2010/nov/07/brazilian-grand-prix-sebastian-vettel|work=Guardian|last=Weaver|first=Paul|location=Interlagos|title=Sebastian Vettel wins Brazilian grand prix to seal title for Red Bull|url-status=live}}</ref>

After the {{F1 GP|2010|Abu Dhabi}}, team boss [[Frank Williams (Formula One)|Frank Williams]] confirmed that Hülkenberg would not be driving for the team in {{F1|2011}}.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.attwilliams.com/news/view/1658|work=[[AT&T]] [[Williams F1|Williams]]|publisher=[[Williams F1]]|location=Grove, United Kingdom|date=15 November 2010|access-date=15 November 2010|title=Barrichello Confirmed for 2011|quote=The team can also confirm that Nico Hulkenberg will not contest the 2011 FIA World Championship with AT&T Williams.|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110707184902/http://www.attwilliams.com/news/view/1658|archive-date=7 July 2011}}</ref>

=== Force India (2011–2012) ===
====2011====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2011 Malaysia FP1.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg as [[Force India]]'s third driver at the [[2011 Malaysian Grand Prix]]]]
On 26 January 2011, Hülkenberg was confirmed as [[Force India]]'s reserve driver for the {{F1|2011}} season, where he would drive for the team in the Friday practice sessions.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/89078|first=Pablo|last=Elizalde|work=[[Autosport]]|date=26 January 2011|access-date=26 January 2011|title=Di Resta confirmed at Force India|archive-date=3 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303195225/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/89078|url-status=live}}</ref> He replaced [[Paul di Resta]], who was promoted to a race position in the team. Hülkenberg competed in the first practice sessions of all the races apart from [[2011 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]], [[2011 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]], [[2011 Korean Grand Prix|Korea]], [[2011 Indian Grand Prix|India]] and [[2011 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]].

====2012====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2012 Malaysia Qualify.jpg|thumb|By the time of the [[2012 Malaysian Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg had been promoted to a full-time race seat.]]
On 16 December 2011, Force India announced di Resta and Hülkenberg would be their drivers for the 2012 season.<ref name="FI2012">{{cite news|url=http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2011/12/12903.html|title=Hulkenberg joins Di Resta in Force India's 2012 line-up|date=16 December 2011|access-date=16 December 2011|publisher=Formula One|archive-date=5 January 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120105144908/http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2011/12/12903.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
Hülkenberg qualified ninth for the {{F1 GP|2012|Australian}}, six places ahead of di Resta, but his race ended on the first lap after picking up damage in a first-corner incident before retiring further round the course. He picked up his first points for Force India the following weekend, at the {{F1 GP|2012|Malaysian}}; he finished in ninth place, having started the race in sixteenth. He again qualified sixteenth, for the {{F1 GP|2012|Chinese}}. He achieved his best Formula One finish at the time with a fourth place at the {{F1 GP|2012|Belgian}}. He had been running 2nd in the race, when he was jumped by [[Kimi Räikkönen]] during the pit stops, before the faster [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] of [[Sebastian Vettel]] passed them both. Hülkenberg did not score any points in [[2012 Italian Grand Prix|Italy]] or [[2012 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]], but collected points at all of the next five {{not a typo|Grands Prix}}, except on the [[2012 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Yas Marina Circuit]] where he was involved in a collision on the first lap, and subsequently retired. This was the first time he scored points in more than two races in a row.

[[File:Nico Hülkenberg, United States Grand Prix, Austin 2012.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg at the [[2012 United States Grand Prix]]]]
In the last race of the season, the [[2012 Brazilian Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg qualified 7th but was promoted to 6th after [[Pastor Maldonado]] received a 10 place grid penalty. By lap three he had moved forward two places and on lap five he passed Fernando Alonso for third place. He moved into second position when McLaren's [[Lewis Hamilton]] pitted on lap 11. Hülkenberg then passed [[Jenson Button]] at the start of lap 19 to take the lead. He and Button built up a 45-second lead before the safety car was deployed because of debris on the track. He still led until he was passed by Hamilton, after sliding at the entry of Turn 8 on lap 49. On lap 55 he collided with Hamilton when the rear of Hülkenberg's car slid out while trying to pass him at Turn 1. This ended Hamilton's last race for McLaren. After being given a drive-through penalty as a result of the incident, Hülkenberg finished fifth - letting his first race win and podium finish slip through his fingers.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenbergs-heart-was-bleeding-after-watching-2012-brazil-f1-race-4984898/4984898/|title=Hulkenberg's heart was bleeding after watching 2012 Brazil F1 race|date=12 December 2019|access-date=27 March 2024|work=Autosport|last=Noble|first=Jonathan|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204085919/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenbergs-heart-was-bleeding-after-watching-2012-brazil-f1-race-4984898/4984898/|archive-date=4 December 2023}}</ref> Nevertheless, this allowed him to take 11th place in the Drivers' Championship from Kamui Kobayashi.

Hülkenberg finished the year 17 points ahead of his teammate Paul di Resta and he out-qualified him 12 times, to di Resta's eight.

=== Sauber (2013) ===
On 31 October 2012, Sauber confirmed that they had signed Hülkenberg for the 2013 season.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/103888|first=Pablo|last=Elizalde|title=Sauber confirms Nico Hulkenberg for 2013 Formula 1 season|work=[[Autosport]]|date=31 October 2012|access-date=2 January 2013|archive-date=7 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160807132427/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/103888|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2013 Malaysia FP2.jpg|thumb|right|Hülkenberg at the [[2013 Malaysian Grand Prix]].]]
Hülkenberg failed to start the {{F1 GP|2013|Australian}} due to a leak in the fuel system of his [[Sauber C32]]; he had qualified eleventh for the race,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.espn.co.uk/australia/motorsport/story/103246.html|title='Disappointed' Hulkenberg misses race|publisher=ESPN F1|date=17 March 2013|access-date=29 March 2013|archive-date=29 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140529090347/http://www.espn.co.uk/australia/motorsport/story/103246.html|url-status=live}}</ref> but was withdrawn for safety reasons.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-motor-racing-prix-hulkenberg-idUKBRE92G02I20130317|title=Sauber's Hulkenberg out of Australian Grand Prix|first=Ian|last=Ransom|date=17 March 2013|access-date=29 March 2013|work=[[Reuters]]|archive-date=17 February 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200217145749/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-motor-racing-prix-hulkenberg-idUKBRE92G02I20130317|url-status=dead}}</ref> At the {{F1 GP|2013|Malaysian}}, Hülkenberg qualified in twelfth, but finished the race in eighth place.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2013/3/14409.html|title=Malaysia: selected team and driver quotes|publisher=Formula One|date=24 March 2013|access-date=29 March 2013|archive-date=5 April 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130405050111/http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2013/3/14409.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg put in his best qualifying performance of the season to date at the [[2013 Italian Grand Prix|Italian Grand Prix]] to put himself 3rd on the grid. After losing two places to the [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferraris]] of [[Felipe Massa]] and Fernando Alonso, he managed to keep 5th place even though harried by the Mercedes of [[Nico Rosberg]] towards the end of the race. By finishing in fifth place, he overtook [[Scuderia Toro Rosso|Toro Rosso]] driver [[Jean-Éric Vergne]] in the Drivers' Championship. His best finish was at the Korean Grand Prix where he finished 4th after close battle with Hamilton and Alonso, in which he showed impressive defending skills and made no mistakes, in a battle where he overtook Hamilton more than once.

=== Return to Force India (2014–2016) ===
====2014====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2014 Singapore FP2.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg at the [[2014 Singapore Grand Prix]]]]
On 3 December 2013, Force India confirmed they had signed Hülkenberg for 2014 to race alongside [[Sergio Pérez]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 November 2013 |title=Hulkenberg and Perez for Force India in 2014 |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-and-perez-for-force-india-in-2014/443583/ |access-date=5 February 2023 |website=www.motorsport.com |language=en |archive-date=5 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230205121729/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-and-perez-for-force-india-in-2014/443583/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the first round, Hülkenberg finished the {{F1 GP|2014|Australian}} in seventh place&nbsp;– his first finish in Melbourne&nbsp;– but was promoted to sixth after the disqualification of second-placed [[Daniel Ricciardo]]. He later finished fifth at the {{F1 GP|2014|Malaysian}}, spending a large amount of time in fourth place, holding off [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]]'s [[Fernando Alonso]] before being overtaken. Another fifth place at Bahrain put Hülkenberg in third place of the drivers' standings, behind the two [[Mercedes-Benz in Formula One|Mercedes]] drivers, [[Lewis Hamilton]] and [[Nico Rosberg]].

At the {{F1 GP|2014|Chinese}}, Hülkenberg took sixth place, taking eight points. He fell to fourth place in the Drivers' Championship after Fernando Alonso finished in third place. Force India lost second in the Constructors' Championship to [[Red Bull Racing]].

Consistent point scoring runs throughout the season meant that Hülkenberg finished the season in 9th place in the Drivers' Championship with 96 points, a career best, compared to his teammate's 59 points and helped Force India achieve sixth place in the Constructors' Championship, following the improvements of Williams and McLaren throughout the season. His best result of the season was fifth place, which he achieved four times.

In October 2014, Force India confirmed they had re-signed Hülkenberg for 2015.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2014/oct/20/nico-hulkenberg-force-india-2015-f1|title=Nico Hülkenberg retained by Force India for 2015 F1 season|work=[[The Guardian]]|agency=[[Press Association]]|date=21 October 2014|access-date=18 November 2014|archive-date=7 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220607165147/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2014/oct/20/nico-hulkenberg-force-india-2015-f1|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2015====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 070615 b.jpg|thumb|Hülkenberg at the [[2015 Canadian Grand Prix]].]]
In the opening round in [[2015 Australian Grand Prix|Australia]], Hülkenberg finished seventh, a lap down. However, he would not score again, in an uncompetitive Force India, until [[2015 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]]. In [[2015 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]], mid-race, he suffered a big crash at turn one when his front wing detached and he drove over it, sending him slightly airborne and into the barriers, costing him a potential fourth place. He then failed to finish five of the next seven races. In [[2015 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]], he had a power unit failure on the way to the grid, while in [[2015 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]], he was tagged by [[Felipe Massa]] at turn three and spun into the barriers, and received a three place grid penalty after being deemed responsible. In [[2015 Russian Grand Prix|Russia]] he spun at turn two at the start and collected [[Marcus Ericsson]], potentially costing him a podium finish, and in the [[2015 United States Grand Prix|United States]] he slid into [[Daniel Ricciardo]] and broke his front right suspension.

Hülkenberg finished the season 10th with 58 points, 20 points behind his teammate, and helped Force India to secure fifth place in the Constructors' Championship and his best result of the season was sixth, which he achieved three times.

====2016====

[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2016 Malaysia FP2 2.jpg|alt=|thumb|left|Hülkenberg at the [[2016 Malaysian Grand Prix]].]]
Hülkenberg again raced for Force India in 2016 alongside [[Sergio Pérez]].

In the opening round in [[2016 Australian Grand Prix|Australia]], Hülkenberg finished seventh. He would finish the next two races in 15th position with the Force India being uncompetitive. In [[2016 Russian Grand Prix|Russia]], he was hit by his former teammate [[Esteban Gutiérrez]] and retired from the race. He would also retire from the next race with an oil leak. A podium, once again, escaped Hülkenberg's grasp in [[2016 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]]. He qualified fifth and was set to finish on the podium, when he got stuck in traffic following his pit stop and his teammate jumped him for the final podium place. He eventually finished sixth, passing the eventual World Champion [[Nico Rosberg]], who had struggled throughout the race, just before the finish line on the last lap. This was followed by points finishes at the next two races. He spun in qualifying during the [[2016 European Grand Prix]] held at Baku, when the Force India was very competitive. This caused him to qualify 13th and finish 9th while Pérez qualified 2nd (demoted to seventh after a grid penalty) and finished third. At the following race in [[2016 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]], he put in another strong qualifying performance to qualify third, which became second when Nico Rosberg served his five-place grid penalty. However, he had a poor start, and was overtaken by quicker cars as the race went on, until his brakes failed and he had to retire. This was followed by five consecutive points finishes, including fourth at the [[2016 Belgian Grand Prix]], his best result of the season, when he was initially running second after the first lap but was eventually passed by the faster cars of [[Daniel Ricciardo]] and [[Lewis Hamilton]]. At the following race in [[2016 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]], he was involved in a first lap collision, where he was squeezed between two drivers, and had to retire from the race. Hülkenberg finished eighth at the next two races. Having announced his decision to move to [[Renault in Formula One|Renault]] for the 2017 season before the [[2016 United States Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg put on some rejuvenated performances. He qualified seventh in the US, before retiring after being squeezed in between [[Valtteri Bottas]] and [[Sebastian Vettel]]. He then qualified fifth in [[2016 Mexico Grand Prix|Mexico]], ahead of the Ferraris and finished seventh. He outqualified his teammate again in Brazil and was running fourth, but he picked up a puncture from debris on the track, following [[Kimi Räikkönen]]'s crash, costing him a podium finish, and fell outside the points before recovering to seventh, fending off Daniel Ricciardo towards the end of the race. He capped off his season in [[2016 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]] by outqualifying Pérez again to seventh place and finishing in the same position, having survived a collision with [[Max Verstappen]] on the first lap.<ref name="formula1.com">{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/championship/drivers/nico-hulkenberg.html|title=Nico Hulkenberg|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=9 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180309231112/https://www.formula1.com/en/championship/drivers/nico-hulkenberg.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="formula1.com1">{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2016/drivers.html|title=Results|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=1 July 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701134749/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2016/drivers.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg scored 72 points in the season, finishing ninth in the Drivers' Championship and helping Force India finish the Constructors' Championship in fourth.<ref name="formula1.com1"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2016/team.html|title=Results|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=1 July 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701134753/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2016/team.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Renault (2017–2019) ===
====2017====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2017 Malaysia FP2 1.jpg|alt=|thumb|Hülkenberg driving for Renault at the [[2017 Malaysian Grand Prix]].]]
On 14 October 2016, Renault Sport announced that Hülkenberg had signed a multiyear agreement to race with the [[Renault in Formula One|Renault Sport Formula One team]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.renaultsport.com/Nico-Hulkenberg-joins-Renault-Sport-Formula-One-Team.html|title=Nico Hülkenberg Joins Renault Sport Formula One Team|work=Renault Sport|date=14 October 2016|access-date=14 October 2016|archive-date=31 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210831003437/https://www.renaultsport.com/Nico-Hulkenberg-joins-Renault-Sport-Formula-One-Team.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg got his first points for the team at the third race of the season in [[2017 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]] with 9th place, followed by 8th in [[2017 Russian Grand Prix|Russia]]. Hülkenberg then finished in 6th place in [[2017 Spanish Grand Prix|Spain]], Renault's best result in the sport since returning in 2016. His point scoring streak ended in [[2017 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]] when he retired, when running in the points, with gearbox issues. He finished 8th in [[2017 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]], which was followed by a retirement in [[2017 Azerbaijan Grand Prix|Azerbaijan]], when he clumsily clipped the wall while running in a promising 6th place. In [[2017 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]], he finished 13th, finishing behind his teammate [[Jolyon Palmer]] for the first time, following a bad start.

A new Renault upgrade brought massive improvement at the [[2017 British Grand Prix]] as Hülkenberg qualified and finished 6th. The car also proved to be the 'best of the rest' (behind Mercedes, Ferrari and Red Bull) in [[2017 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]] as he qualified 7th, but a 5 place grid penalty for a new gearbox meant that he started 12th on the grid. He was set to score points in the race, before a slow pit stop dropped him down the field and he eventually retired from the race. There was an incident during the race, when [[Kevin Magnussen]] had pushed Hülkenberg off the track as they were battling for position. Magnussen received a time penalty for the incident. Hülkenberg rudely confronted Magnussen while he was interviewed by Danish TV in the media pen, labelling him 'nasty' and the 'most unsporting driver of the whole grid' and Magnussen calmly responded with 'suck my balls, mate'.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/131038/hulkenberg-and-magnussen-in-postrace-row|title=Nico Hulkenberg and Kevin Magnussen row after Hungarian GP clash|first=Valentin|last=Khorounzhiy|website=Autosport.com|date=30 July 2017 |access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=1 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230701110711/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/nico-hulkenberg-and-kevin-magnussen-row-after-hungarian-gp-clash-4998192/4998192/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/motor-racing/Formula1/hungarian-grand-prix-kevin-magnussen-nico-hulkenberg-suck-my-balls-clash-interview-video-a7867906.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220620/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/motor-racing/Formula1/hungarian-grand-prix-kevin-magnussen-nico-hulkenberg-suck-my-balls-clash-interview-video-a7867906.html |archive-date=20 June 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Kevin Magnussen tells Nico Hülkenberg to 'suck my balls' in ugly post-race clash|date=30 July 2017|website=Independent.co.uk|access-date=18 October 2017}}</ref> Hülkenberg entered the summer break with 26 points and in 10th place in the championship.

Hülkenberg returned from the summer break in [[2017 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]] in good form as he qualified 7th and finished 6th in the race. At [[2017 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]], it was announced that [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]] would replace Jolyon Palmer, who at that time had scored 0 points to Hülkenberg's 34 points, for the 2018 season. Hülkenberg qualified 'best of the rest' in 7th. After the first lap of the race, Hülkenberg found himself in 3rd place, following the first corner collision and subsequent retirements of [[Sebastian Vettel]], [[Kimi Räikkönen]], [[Max Verstappen]] and the fast-starting McLaren of [[Fernando Alonso]]. He looked set to take his long-awaited first podium finish in Formula 1, until [[Daniil Kvyat]] crashed and brought out the Safety Car and a blunder in strategy by Renault left him in 5th place. He eventually got up to 4th place, before an oil leak brought about his retirement from the race, in which he succeeded [[Adrian Sutil]] to become the record holder for the most starts in Formula 1 without a podium finish.

At the [[2017 Japanese Grand Prix|Japanese Grand Prix]], it was announced that Carlos Sainz Jr. would replace Palmer for the rest of the season beginning at the next Grand Prix in Austin.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2017/10/f1-sainz-palmer-renault-austin-toro-rosso-kvyat-usa.html|title=Sainz to replace Palmer at Renault from Austin|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=3 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180803224126/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2017/10/f1-sainz-palmer-renault-austin-toro-rosso-kvyat-usa.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In the race, Hülkenberg was running comfortably in the points for the majority of the race until a failure in the DRS mechanism of his car forced him to retire from the race. In their time together as teammates Hülkenberg scored 34 points to Palmer's 8 points and outqualified Palmer in all 16 races.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2017/drivers/NICHUL01/nico-hulkenberg.html|title=Results|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=8 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171008180407/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2017/drivers/NICHUL01/nico-hulkenberg.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Nico Hülkenberg">{{cite web|url=http://en.espn.co.uk/renault/motorsport/driver/2790.html|title=Nico Hülkenberg|website=ESPN UK|access-date=18 October 2017|archive-date=18 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171018050642/http://en.espn.co.uk/renault/motorsport/driver/2790.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="formula1.com" />
In the [[2017 United States Grand Prix|USA]], Hülkenberg retired on lap 4 with an engine issue.<ref name="Nico Hülkenberg" /> In [[2017 Mexican Grand Prix|Mexico]], he once again retired, again from a net 4th place, with an engine issue. This was the third time in a row that he had failed to finish a race and the fourth time in five races. At the [[2017 Brazilian Grand Prix|Brazilian Grand Prix]], he led home teammate Sainz to 10th place, his first points in almost 3 months, with his previous points finish being his 6th-place finish at Belgium in August.
He qualified 'best of the rest' in [[2017 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]] with 7th place. He finished the race in 6th place, having received a contentious 5-second penalty for passing [[Sergio Pérez]] off the track at the start of the race, when many believed he should have given the position back. He ended up building enough of a gap to Pérez that the time penalty did not affect his position. With 6th place, Renault overtook Toro Rosso in the Constructors' Championship for 6th place.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2017/11/race-bottas-holds-off-hamilton-in-2017-finale.html|title=Race - Bottas defeats Hamilton in 2017 finale|website=Formula1.com|access-date=2 December 2017|archive-date=1 December 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171201034130/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2017/11/race-bottas-holds-off-hamilton-in-2017-finale.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

This confirmed Renault's position as 6th in the Constructors' Championship and moved Hülkenberg up to 43 points for the season, the same as Massa, but courtesy of more 6th-place finishes, he ended the season 10th in the standings. He had outqualified his teammates over the course of the season 19-1 and outscored his teammates 43–14 over the course of the season.

====2018====
For the 2018 season, Hülkenberg remained at Renault, alongside Carlos Sainz, who was competing in his first full season for the Renault F1 team.
[[File:Niko Hulkenberg-Test Days 2018 Circuit Barcelona (3).jpg|alt=|thumb|left|Hülkenberg in the pre-season testing, Barcelona]]
Hülkenberg and Renault started the season well with a 7th and two 6th places in the first 3 races. The first race in [[2018 Australian Grand Prix|Australia]] with Sainz finishing in 10th, marked the first double points finish for Renault in F1 since the [[2011 Turkish Grand Prix]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2018/races/979/australia.html|title=Results|website=Formula1.com|access-date=6 April 2018|archive-date=23 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220523115550/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2018/races/979/australia.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/4/race--ricciardo-wins-wild-race-in-china-for-red-bull.html|title=RACE: Ricciardo wins wild Chinese Grand Prix for Red Bull|website=Formula1.com|access-date=17 April 2018|archive-date=31 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180831035410/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/4/race--ricciardo-wins-wild-race-in-china-for-red-bull.html|url-status=live}}</ref> At the fourth race of the season in [[2018 Azerbaijan Grand Prix|Baku]], Hülkenberg was handed a 5 place grid penalty for changing his damaged gearbox. He qualified 9th and would start 14th. This meant the end of his streak of starting the last 6 races 7th on the grid. He had made his way up to 5th place by lap 10, when he lost the rear of his car on lap 10, and clattered the wall with his left rear tyre and retired with suspension damage, ending his 5 race point-scoring streak. It was second time in two years that he had retired from a top 6 position in Baku from an unforced error.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/abiteboul-baku-hulkenbergs-one-mistake-for-the-year.html|title=Abiteboul – Baku Hulkenberg's one mistake for 2018|website=Formula1.com|access-date=17 May 2018|archive-date=31 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180831035421/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/abiteboul-baku-hulkenbergs-one-mistake-for-the-year.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:2018 Spanish Grand Prix Hulkenberg (5).jpg|alt=|thumb|Hülkenberg at the [[2018 Spanish Grand Prix]].]]
Hülkenberg's misfortune continued at the next race in [[2018 Spanish Grand Prix|Spain]]. He went out in Q1 for the first time in 59 races due to a fuel pressure problem with his car. Then he was taken out by the spinning [[Romain Grosjean]] on the first lap of the race.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/race--spain-hamilton-cruises-to-mercedes-one-two.html|title=RACE: Hamilton heads Mercedes 1-2, as Verstappen edges Vettel|website=Formula1.com|access-date=17 May 2018|archive-date=15 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180515093627/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/race--spain-hamilton-cruises-to-mercedes-one-two.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg criticised Grosjean's driving following the crash, for which the Frenchman received a grid penalty for the next race.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/grosjean-_likes-spinning-a-lot--hulkenberg-after-lap-1-crash.html|title=Grosjean penalised for triggering first-lap shunt|website=Formula1.com|access-date=17 May 2018|archive-date=31 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180831035351/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/5/grosjean-_likes-spinning-a-lot--hulkenberg-after-lap-1-crash.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In [[2018 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]], Hülkenberg was outqualified by a teammate by merit for the first time since the [[2016 Japanese Grand Prix]]. Nevertheless, he finished the race in 8th, having started in 11th, with his teammate Sainz obeying team orders late in the race to let him past.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2018/races/984/monaco.html|title=Formula 1 Grand Prix de Monaco 2018 - Race Result|website=Formula1.com|access-date=30 May 2018|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162709/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2018/races/984/monaco.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He followed this up with another point-scoring finish in [[2018 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]] after qualifying and finishing the race in 7th.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/6/race--vettel-wins-in-canada-to-seize-championship-lead.html|title=RACE: Vettel wins in Canada to seize championship lead|website=Formula1.com|access-date=11 June 2018|archive-date=30 July 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180730165815/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/6/race--vettel-wins-in-canada-to-seize-championship-lead.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The next three races made up Formula One's first ever triple header in [[2018 French Grand Prix|France]], [[2018 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]] and [[2018 British Grand Prix|Britain]]. Hülkenberg finished 9th in France, followed by a retirement from 9th in Austria with an engine failure. It was his 3rd retirement in 6 races and his 7th retirement in his last 16 races. However, he finished the triple header on a high, finishing 6th at the [[2018 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]]. Hülkenberg achieved his best ever result for Renault at his home grand prix in [[2018 German Grand Prix|Germany]] with 5th place, overtaking [[Kevin Magnussen]] late on when the rain started to fall. After a refuelling problem limited him to only 13th on the grid, he finished 12th at the [[2018 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian Grand Prix]]. Nevertheless, he entered the summer break with 52 points and 7th in the championship as the effective leader of the midfield, which many drivers had started to dub the B championship of Formula 1 or 'Formula 1.5', due to very large gap in performance to the top three teams of Mercedes, Ferrari and Red Bull.

In [[2018 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]], Hülkenberg triggered a massive first corner collision after starting 18th due to engine penalties.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/8/hulkenberg-given-10-place-italian-gp-grid-penalty-for-spa-start-.html|title=Hulkenberg given 10-place Italian GP grid penalty for Spa start crash|website=Formula1.com|access-date=17 November 2018|archive-date=31 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180831072105/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/8/hulkenberg-given-10-place-italian-gp-grid-penalty-for-spa-start-.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg outbraked himself and hit Fernando Alonso, who was launched above the Sauber of [[Charles Leclerc (racing driver)|Charles Leclerc]], with one of Alonso's tyres hitting the new controversially introduced safety device, the halo. The collision also ultimately ended the races of both Kimi Räikkönen and Daniel Ricciardo. Hülkenberg was deemed to have 'completely misjudged the situation' by the race stewards and handed a 10 place grid penalty for the next race. The spectacular crash was likened to the crash at the [[2012 Belgian Grand Prix]] initiated by [[Romain Grosjean]]. Following the incident, Hülkenberg who had been a vocal opposer to the halo, introduced in 2018, admitted that it was 'pretty useful'.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/45359816|title=Halo: Nico Hulkenberg admits he was wrong about 'pretty useful' F1 safety device|date=30 August 2018|access-date=17 November 2018|website=Bbc.co.uk|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528202804/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/45359816|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg suffered a tough period of results, coinciding with Renault's loss of competitiveness, by only scoring 1 point in the next 4 races. He bounced back with a 6th-place finish at the [[2018 United States Grand Prix|United States Grand Prix]]. With Sainz finishing in 7th, this was the best team result for Renault in a race since they rejoined the sport in 2016, beating the 7th and 8th place the two drivers had achieved in Canada earlier in the year. This was followed by another strong race in [[2018 Mexican Grand Prix|Mexico]] with another 6th place. His season ended with two successive retirements due to high engine temperatures in [[2018 Brazilian Grand Prix|Brazil]] and after being rolled over into the barriers by Romain Grosjean in [[2018 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]].

Nevertheless, Hülkenberg finished the season as the "champion of the rest" in 7th place with 69 points, 7 points ahead of [[Sergio Pérez]], as Renault also secured 4th place in the Constructor's Championship.

====2019====
[[File:2019 Formula One tests Barcelona, Hülkenberg (40348248973).jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg driving during 2019 pre-season testing]]
For the 2019 season, Hülkenberg was joined at Renault by multiple Grand Prix winner [[Daniel Ricciardo]], who was signed from Red Bull. Hülkenberg, yet to score a podium in 158 races by the beginning of the 2019 season, said his "future in the sport depends on the outcome of the duel" as his ability could be measured "against a Grand Prix winner", for the first time since his rookie season in 2010, when he was teammate to [[Rubens Barrichello]] at Williams. Meanwhile, Renault managing director, [[Cyril Abiteboul]], believed that the driver pairing was "one of the strongest - if not the strongest - driver line-up on the grid".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.express.co.uk/sport/f1-autosport/1087116/Renault-Nico-Hulkenberg-Daniel-Ricciardo-Formula-One-news|title=F1 news: Nico Hulkenberg makes Daniel Ricciardo admission after Aussie joins Renault|first=Joshua|last=Peck|date=14 February 2019|website=Express.co.uk|access-date=18 March 2019|archive-date=2 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220602150117/https://www.express.co.uk/sport/f1-autosport/1087116/Renault-Nico-Hulkenberg-Daniel-Ricciardo-Formula-One-news|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg started the season strongly, outqualifying new teammate Ricciardo at his [[2019 Australian Grand Prix|home race]], but an engine issue prevented him from reaching Q3, leaving him 11th. He made a very strong start and finished the race in 7th.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/renault-ricciardo-debut-mainly-lows/4355530/|title=Renault: Ricciardo's debut 'a weekend mainly of lows'|website=Motorsport.com|date=19 March 2019 |access-date=18 April 2019|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162709/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/renault-ricciardo-debut-mainly-lows/4355530/|url-status=live}}</ref> However, he was hit by misfortune in the next race in [[2019 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]]. An engine mapping issue restricted him to 17th in qualifying.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/47760616|title=Bahrain Grand Prix: Ferrari's Charles Leclerc takes maiden pole position|date=30 March 2019|access-date=18 April 2019|website=Bbc.co.uk|archive-date=23 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220423203807/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/47760616|url-status=live}}</ref> However, he had a fantastic race, moving up 11 places and being on course to finish 6th, surviving contact with Ricciardo on the way, when the Australian slid into him as Hülkenberg overtook him. Then, on lap 53, 4 laps from the end, disaster struck as both Hülkenberg and Ricciardo, running 6th and 10th respectively, both retired with engine issues at the same corner.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.ricciardo-double-dnf-heartbreaking-for-renault.410xAtFuMxTtrP64UHXICY.html|title=Bahrain Grand Prix 2019: Ricciardo: Double DNF 'heartbreaking' for Renault|website=Formula1.com|access-date=18 April 2019|archive-date=26 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526042859/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.ricciardo-double-dnf-heartbreaking-for-renault.410xAtFuMxTtrP64UHXICY.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Abiteboul stated that Renault's engine problems were "unacceptable".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/11681370/renaults-engine-problems-are-unacceptable-says-cyril-abiteboul|title=Renault's engine problems are 'unacceptable', says Cyril Abiteboul|website=Skysports.com|access-date=18 April 2019|archive-date=29 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220529192115/https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/11681370/renaults-engine-problems-are-unacceptable-says-cyril-abiteboul|url-status=live}}</ref> These issues were compounded, when Hülkenberg again retired from a points-scoring position with engine issues at the next race in [[2019 Chinese Grand Prix|China]], this time with a software issue.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-renault-giving-away-points/4372321/|title=Hulkenberg: Renault giving away points with poor reliability|website=Motorsport.com|date=17 April 2019 |access-date=18 April 2019|archive-date=3 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603232321/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-renault-giving-away-points/4372321/|url-status=live}}</ref> In [[2019 Spanish Grand Prix|Spain]], Hülkenberg crashed in qualifying and damaged his front wing. He had to fit a different specification front wing, which broke parc fermé rules, ensuring he would have to start the race in the pit lane. He would finish the race in 13th.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.crash.net/f1/news/919856/1/hulkenberg-start-spanish-gp-pit-lane|title=Hulkenberg to start Spanish GP from pit lane|date=12 May 2019|website=Crash|access-date=15 May 2019|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528191316/https://www.crash.net/f1/news/919856/1/hulkenberg-start-spanish-gp-pit-lane|url-status=live}}</ref> In the following race in [[2019 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]], a chance of a points finish evaporated, when the Ferrari of [[Charles Leclerc]] hit Hülkenberg, while attempting an overtaking move, which the German labelled "too ambitious". Both drivers suffered punctures. Hülkenberg recovered to 13th, but Leclerc eventually retired from the race.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/143684/leclerc-too-impatient-says-hulkenberg-after-clash|title=Charles Leclerc "too impatient" in Monaco clash - Nico Hulkenberg|first1=Stuart|last1=Codling|first2=Valentin|last2=Khorounzhiy|website=Autosport.com|date=26 May 2019 |access-date=27 May 2019|archive-date=25 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225142203/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/143684/leclerc-too-impatient-says-hulkenberg-after-clash|url-status=live}}</ref> He then finished the next two races in 7th and 8th place in [[2019 Canadian Grand Prix|Canada]] and [[2019 French Grand Prix|France]] respectively. At the [[2019 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]], he recovered to a 10th-place finish, after he was hit by former teammate Sergio Pérez and his engine momentarily failing and going into 'limp mode', whilst also bemoaning the team's strategy.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-frustrated-that-renault-made-life-difficult-at-silverstone.6ltjMEfjYOK8IIdjrUEmdl.html|title=Hulkenberg frustrated that Renault 'made life difficult for themselves' at Silverstone|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162710/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-frustrated-that-renault-made-life-difficult-at-silverstone.6ltjMEfjYOK8IIdjrUEmdl.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg then crashed out of his [[2019 German Grand Prix|home race]] in precarious wet conditions. He was in 4th place, having run as high as 2nd at one point, when he crashed out at Turn 16, where fellow drivers Charles Leclerc, race leader Lewis Hamilton and former teammate Carlos Sainz also went off. The latter two managed to survive their incidents and continue, while both Leclerc and Hülkenberg retired. Both retirees criticised the run-off of the corner, which is a different tarmac used for dragster racing, which meant there was no grip in the wet conditions to help prevent the accident.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.racefans.net/2019/08/02/hulkenberg-hockenheim-drag-strip-run-off-not-up-to-f1-standard/|title=Hulkenberg: Hockenheim drag strip run-off not up to F1 standard|date=2 August 2019|website=www.racefans.net|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=8 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108001457/https://www.racefans.net/2019/08/02/hulkenberg-hockenheim-drag-strip-run-off-not-up-to-f1-standard/|url-status=live}}</ref> At the following race in [[2019 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]], Hülkenberg was in the running for points, but then he suffered yet another engine problem on his Renault, which meant he was only able to finish in 12th place.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/renault-hulkenberg-safe-mode-ricciardo/4508331/|title=Hulkenberg's Hungarian GP compromised by engine 'safe mode'|website=www.motorsport.com|date=4 August 2019 |access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=5 August 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190805103116/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/renault-hulkenberg-safe-mode-ricciardo/4508331/|url-status=live}}</ref> Going into the summer break, Hülkenberg was only 14th in the championship with 17 points, 5 points behind new teammate Daniel Ricciardo in 11th place. Renault's points total of 39 points was 43 points behind the position they were in the previous year, after they suffered a disappointing first half of the season.

Just before the [[2019 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgian Grand Prix]], Renault announced that Hülkenberg would be replaced by Mercedes reserve driver and former Force India driver [[Esteban Ocon]] for the 2020 season.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/49509020|title=Esteban Ocon to replace Nico Hulkenberg at Renault for 2020 F1 season|work=BBC Sport|date=29 August 2019|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=28 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528220919/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/49509020|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg said the decision was "not only about performance", alluding that the French Renault team desired a French driver in Ocon.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.racefans.net/2019/08/30/hulkenberg-nationality-factor-behind-renaults-decision-to-replace-him-with-ocon/|title=Hulkenberg: Nationality a factor in Renault's move for Ocon|date=30 August 2019|website=www.racefans.net|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=27 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527121311/https://www.racefans.net/2019/08/30/hulkenberg-nationality-factor-behind-renaults-decision-to-replace-him-with-ocon/|url-status=live}}</ref> Four-time World Champion [[Alain Prost]], non-executive director for Renault Sport at the time, said that Renault offered Hülkenberg a new one-year contract with an option for another year, but the German refused the offer due to wanting a full two-year contract.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/renault-nico-hulkenberg-one-year-deal/|title=Renault offered Nico Hulkenberg new one-year deal|date=31 August 2019|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=22 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422162709/https://www.planetf1.com/news/renault-nico-hulkenberg-one-year-deal/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg started the second half of the season strongly by qualifying 7th in Belgium. A grid penalty meant he started 12th, and he fell even further back when he took evasive action to avoid collisions involving Verstappen, Räikkönen, Ricciardo and Stroll. However, he executed an alternate two-stop strategy and made up 3 places in the last lap to finish 8th.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.leclerc-holds-off-hamilton-to-take-emotional-maiden-grand-prix-victory.7peJjlJIqpoCQC8cT24u23.html|title=Belgian Grand Prix 2019 F1 race report: Leclerc holds off Hamilton to take maiden Grand Prix victory|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=2 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190902052859/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.leclerc-holds-off-hamilton-to-take-emotional-maiden-grand-prix-victory.7peJjlJIqpoCQC8cT24u23.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In [[2019 Italian Grand Prix|Italy]], both Renaults had a very strong weekend, with Ricciardo and Hülkenberg qualifying 5th and 6th respectively, with both drivers moving up a place to finish 4th and 5th, after Sebastian Vettel spun out of contention. This was Renault's best points haul since returning to the sport in 2016, and Hülkenberg's joint best result for the team.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/opinion/f1/2019-italian-grand-prix-race-report-steely-leclercs-monza-masterclass|title=2019 Italian Grand Prix race report: steely Leclerc's Monza masterclass|date=9 September 2019|website=Motor Sport Magazine|access-date=18 September 2019|archive-date=1 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230701110701/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/f1/opinion/|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:2019 Japanese Grand Prix Nico Hülkenberg (49028198968).jpg|alt=|thumb|Hülkenberg at the [[2019 Japanese Grand Prix]].]]
In [[2019 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]], Hülkenberg had initially qualified 9th but as teammate Daniel Ricciardo had been disqualified from qualifying as his car exceeded the MGU-K power limit, he started the race in 8th. In the race, Hülkenberg was involved in a first lap collision with former teammate [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]] Both drivers suffered punctures and had to pit on the first lap and fell to the back of the field. However, he managed to recover to 9th place for his 3rd points finish in a row.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/146176/sainz-reckons-hulkenberg-regrets-firstlap-move|title=Sainz reckons Hulkenberg regrets Singapore GP first-lap move|first=Edd Straw, Erwin Jaeggi, Jake|last=Boxall-Legge|website=Autosport.com|date=22 September 2019 |access-date=2 November 2019|archive-date=23 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190923084904/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/146176/sainz-reckons-hulkenberg-regrets-firstlap-move|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg started the [[2019 Russian Grand Prix|next race]] in an impressive 6th place. However, his race was compromised by a bad start which left him outside the top 10. Having recovered to 9th place, the Renault pit crew dropped his car off the jack, which again dropped him out of the top 10. He eventually recovered to finish in 10th, overtaking Lance Stroll towards the end of the race. After the race, Hülkenberg said that "everything that could go wrong did go wrong".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.everything-that-could-go-wrong-did-go-wrong-hulkenberg.2z4eNs449sYhhhzEo5zdps.html|title=Russian Grand Prix 2019: 'Everything that could go wrong did go wrong' – Hulkenberg|website=www.formula1.com|access-date=2 November 2019|archive-date=31 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531194248/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.everything-that-could-go-wrong-did-go-wrong-hulkenberg.2z4eNs449sYhhhzEo5zdps.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Further misfortune hit Hülkenberg in [[2019 Japanese Grand Prix|Japan]]. He was restricted to only 15th in qualifying, after a mechanical failure in Q2 meant he could not set a representative time to progress further. However, in the race he made a fantastic start and was up to 10th place by the end of the first lap and he would end up finishing the race in that position.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.what-the-teams-said-race-day-in-japan-2019.XUdMsTX4ncSmmzKXLdZOU.html|title=What the teams said - Qualifying and race day in Japan &#124; Formula 1®|website=www.formula1.com|access-date=2 November 2019|archive-date=14 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191014103059/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.what-the-teams-said-race-day-in-japan-2019.XUdMsTX4ncSmmzKXLdZOU.html|url-status=live}}</ref> However, following a protest by rival constructor [[Racing Point F1 Team|Racing Point]], both Renault cars were disqualified for having a pre-set automated brake bias system that was deemed to be a driver aid, and thus illegal.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.renault-disqualified-from-results-of-the-japanese-grand-prix.5lDaMU1SWKaXnpEP93CLVB.html|title=Renault disqualified from results of the Japanese Grand Prix &#124; Formula 1®|website=www.formula1.com|access-date=2 November 2019|archive-date=23 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191023201338/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.renault-disqualified-from-results-of-the-japanese-grand-prix.5lDaMU1SWKaXnpEP93CLVB.html|url-status=live}}</ref> This was Hülkenberg's first disqualification in his entire F1 career.

Hülkenberg then managed to salvage a point at the [[2019 Mexican Grand Prix|following race]] with 10th after he was spun into the wall by [[Daniil Kvyat]] at the last corner of the last lap, when running in 9th. He crossed the line in 11th, without a rear wing, but was promoted to 10th after Kvyat was penalised for the incident.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.kvyat-handed-post-race-penalty-loses-points-after-hulkenberg-clash.2BPqIj1u0tcDgn75I28fXR.html|title=Kvyat handed post-race time penalty, loses points after Hulkenberg clash|website=www.formula1.com|access-date=26 November 2019|archive-date=28 October 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191028052515/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.kvyat-handed-post-race-penalty-loses-points-after-hulkenberg-clash.2BPqIj1u0tcDgn75I28fXR.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He followed this up with yet another points finish in the [[2019 United States Grand Prix|USA]] with 9th place.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.gpblog.com/en/news/49552/nico-hulkenberg-pleased-with-his-performance.html|title=Nico Hulkenberg "pleased" with his performance|website=www.gpblog.com|date=5 November 2019 |access-date=26 November 2019|archive-date=18 October 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211018140528/https://www.gpblog.com/en/news/49552/nico-hulkenberg-pleased-with-his-performance.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He suffered a difficult race in [[2019 Brazilian Grand Prix|Brazil]], where two safety car periods interfered in his strategy and left him 12th on the line. He was penalised for overtaking [[Kevin Magnussen]] before the safety car line during the safety car restart, and thus was classified 15th, his worst result of the year.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.racefans.net/2019/11/21/2019-brazilian-grand-prix-star-performers/ |title=2019 Brazilian Grand Prix Star Performers |date=21 November 2019 |website=www.racefans.net |access-date=26 November 2019 |archive-date=25 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210725094734/https://www.racefans.net/2019/11/21/2019-brazilian-grand-prix-star-performers/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He was voted Driver of the Day in the season finale in [[2019 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]],  taking the title for the first time, after finishing 12th in what was his possible final race in Formula One, having failed to secure a drive for the 2020 season.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-hulkenberg-bows-out-with-prize-drive-for-renault.63L6w1x0i5dbW8SDZ6QEtB.html|title=Driver of the Day: Hulkenberg bows out with prize drive for Renault|website=www.formula1.com|access-date=29 December 2019|archive-date=5 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220605154050/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-hulkenberg-bows-out-with-prize-drive-for-renault.63L6w1x0i5dbW8SDZ6QEtB.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/147278/hulkenberg-doesnt-feel-like-hell-be-leaving-f1|title=Hulkenberg doesn't feel like he'll be leaving F1 after losing seat|first=Adam|last=Cooper|website=Autosport.com|date=21 November 2019 |access-date=29 December 2019|archive-date=28 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028014824/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/147278/hulkenberg-doesnt-feel-like-hell-be-leaving-f1|url-status=live}}</ref>

Hülkenberg finished the season in 14th place, his lowest placing in the standings in the sport since his debut season in 2010, having scored 37 points across the season, 17 less than teammate Daniel Ricciardo.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.sport24.co.za/Motorsport/hulkenberg-relieved-latest-f1-spell-over-20191201|title=Hulkenberg 'relieved' latest F1 spell over|date=1 December 2019|website=Sport24|access-date=29 December 2019|archive-date=30 December 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191230090442/https://www.sport24.co.za/Motorsport/hulkenberg-relieved-latest-f1-spell-over-20191201|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Racing Point / Aston Martin reserve driver (2020–2022) ===
Hülkenberg did not have a contract for the 2020 F1 season. He replaced [[Sergio Pérez]] at [[Racing Point]] for the [[2020 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]] and [[70th Anniversary Grand Prix]] after Pérez tested positive for [[Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2|SARS-2 coronavirus]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/12039613/nico-hulkenberg-replaces-sergio-perez-at-racing-point-for-british-gp|title=Nico Hülkenberg replaces Sergio Perez at Racing Point for British GP|publisher=Sky Sports F1|website=skysports.com|access-date=31 July 2020|archive-date=18 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818040501/https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/12039613/nico-hulkenberg-replaces-sergio-perez-at-racing-point-for-british-gp|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-silverstone-perez-covid-positive/4850976/?ic_source=home-page-widget&ic_medium=widget&ic_campaign=widget-6|title=F1 news: Hulkenberg returns as Perez tests COVID positive again|date=7 August 2020 |access-date=7 August 2020|archive-date=18 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200818040501/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-silverstone-perez-covid-positive/4850976/?ic_source=home-page-widget&ic_medium=widget&ic_campaign=widget-6|url-status=live}}</ref> For the British Grand Prix he qualified thirteenth but did not start the race due to an engine failure before the start of the race.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nico Hulkenberg fails to make British GP start after last-minute technical issue on his F1 return {{!}} Formula 1®|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-fails-to-make-british-gp-start-after-last-minute-technical-issue.3LeAfHcnAKt5KhqxFjDB82.html|access-date=2 August 2020|website=www.formula1.com|language=en|archive-date=3 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210103023852/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-fails-to-make-british-gp-start-after-last-minute-technical-issue.3LeAfHcnAKt5KhqxFjDB82.html|url-status=live}}</ref> At the [[70th Anniversary Grand Prix]], he surprisingly qualified in third behind the two Mercedes cars and was running in 4th in the race. However, due to unexpectedly high levels of tyre wear, he was forced to pit again. He then went on to finish seventh behind teammate [[Lance Stroll]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=8 August 2020|title=Hulkenberg 'didn't expect' P3 on the grid – but fears race rustiness could 'hurt' first F1 podium chances|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-didnt-expect-p3-on-the-grid-but-fears-race-rustiness-could-hurt.0pszXuT4nIO5DkC0XKHDY.html|access-date=8 August 2020|website=formula1.com|archive-date=12 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200812144013/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.hulkenberg-didnt-expect-p3-on-the-grid-but-fears-race-rustiness-could-hurt.0pszXuT4nIO5DkC0XKHDY.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg did not race at the [[2020 Spanish Grand Prix|Spanish Grand Prix]] as Pérez returned to racing after testing negative for COVID-19.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/53763779| title=Sergio Perez: Racing Point driver to return at Spanish Grand Prix after negative Covid-19 test| work=[[BBC Sport]]| date=13 August 2020| access-date=13 August 2020| archive-date=23 May 2022| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220523143455/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/53763779| url-status=live}}</ref> He would later replace Stroll for the [[Eifel Grand Prix]], after the Canadian had sat out of the final practice session due to him feeling unwell before later testing positive for COVID-19 himself. Hülkenberg finished the race in 8th after qualifying 20th, and last, on the grid, winning him the fans' vote of "Driver of the Day".<ref name="ReferenceA">{{Cite web|date=10 October 2020|title=Super sub Hulkenberg to replace unwell Stroll for remainder of Eifel GP weekend|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-super-sub-hulkenberg-drafted-in-to-replace-unwell-stroll-eifel.1wem3IoVVPhHijg8h3f041.html|access-date=10 October 2020|website=Formula1.com|archive-date=23 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023223845/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-super-sub-hulkenberg-drafted-in-to-replace-unwell-stroll-eifel.1wem3IoVVPhHijg8h3f041.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Eifel GP results|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2020/races/1055/germany/race-result.html|access-date=11 October 2020|archive-date=8 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200808151419/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2020/races/1055/germany/race-result.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=11 October 2020|title=Hulkenberg delighted with 'totally unexpected' P8 on one-off return in Germany|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-hulkenberg-delighted-with-totally-unexpected-p8-in-germany.76Uidt1BtaLYg3VL7ZgxyD.html|access-date=11 October 2020|archive-date=31 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531065702/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.driver-of-the-day-hulkenberg-delighted-with-totally-unexpected-p8-in-germany.76Uidt1BtaLYg3VL7ZgxyD.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Racing Point, now [[Aston Martin in Formula One|Aston Martin Cognizant Formula One Team]], signed Hülkenberg as a reserve and development driver for the {{f1|2021}} season following his stand-in performances in the prior season.<ref>{{Cite web |author= Benson, Andrew |title= Formula 1: Nico Hulkenberg joins Aston Martin as reserve & development driver for 2021 |url= https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/56674921 |date= 8 April 2021 |access-date= 8 April 2021 |website= BBC Sport |archive-date= 27 May 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220527065007/https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/56674921 |url-status= live }}</ref>

After retaining his role as the team's reserve driver in {{f1|2022}}, Hülkenberg replaced [[Sebastian Vettel]] at the season opening [[2022 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain Grand Prix]] after Vettel tested positive for [[COVID-19]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Vettel to be replaced by Hulkenberg for 2022 Bahrain GP after positive Covid test |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-vettel-to-be-replaced-by-hulkenberg-for-2022-bahrain-gp-after.5pgESYwenU9KPErL3MfFRz.html |website=www.formula1.com |publisher=Formula One Management |access-date=17 March 2022 |archive-date=16 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220416192213/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-vettel-to-be-replaced-by-hulkenberg-for-2022-bahrain-gp-after.5pgESYwenU9KPErL3MfFRz.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Hülkenberg started and finished the race in 17th place. Hülkenberg returned to Aston Martin in the following race, the [[2022 Saudi Arabian Grand Prix|Saudi Arabian Grand Prix]] starting in 17th and finishing in 12th place.

Hülkenberg got back into the Aston Martin in the post-[[2022 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian Grand Prix]] Pirelli tests, alongside [[Lance Stroll]] to develop the 2023 tyres.<ref>{{Cite web |title=F1: Nico Hulkenberg volta a pilotar o Aston Martin na Hungria {{!}} AutoSport |url=https://www.autosport.pt/formula1/f1/f1-nico-hulkenberg-volta-a-pilotar-o-aston-martin-na-hungria/ |access-date=2 August 2022 |website=www.autosport.pt |date=2 August 2022 |archive-date=2 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220802171114/https://www.autosport.pt/formula1/f1/f1-nico-hulkenberg-volta-a-pilotar-o-aston-martin-na-hungria/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Haas (2023–2024) ===
====2023====
[[File:FIA F1 Austria 2023 Nr. 27 (1).jpg|thumb|Hülkenberg at the [[2023 Austrian Grand Prix]]]]
In November 2022, [[Haas F1 Team]] announced that Hülkenberg had signed to drive for them in [[2023 Formula One World Championship|2023]], partnering [[Kevin Magnussen]] and replacing fellow German compatriot [[Mick Schumacher]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nico Hulkenberg to make full-time racing return to Formula 1 with Haas in 2023|date=17 November 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-nico-hulkenberg-to-make-full-time-racing-return-to-formula-1-with.1ZoycJ2wgYF82Vt7A2o3xF.html|access-date=17 November 2022|website=[[Formula 1]]|language=en|archive-date=17 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221117053642/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-nico-hulkenberg-to-make-full-time-racing-return-to-formula-1-with.1ZoycJ2wgYF82Vt7A2o3xF.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The first race at the {{F1 GP|2023|Bahrain}} saw Hülkenberg qualify tenth and finish fifteenth, behind his teammate Magnussen. He had also picked up a fifteen-second penalty for exceeding track limits, which ultimately did not affect his final position in the race.<ref name="Results Bahrain 23">{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2023/races/1141/bahrain/race-result.html|title=Formula 1 Gulf Air Bahrain Grand Prix 2023 – Race Result|website=Formula 1|date=5 March 2023|access-date=15 March 2023|archive-date=8 March 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230308203407/https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2023/races/1141/bahrain/race-result.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In the [[2023 Australian Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg benefitted from the turn one incident in lap 57, running in fourth when the third of three red flags during the race was shown; however, he was demoted back to seventh after it was determined that the drivers would return to their previous positions before the final red flag. Haas lodged an unsuccessful protest after the race.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-haas-protest-results-of-2023-australian-grand-prix.2VIj7H2Oe3V3AAPGESJOdd.html|title=Stewards dismiss Haas protest over provisional results of Australian Grand Prix|date=2 April 2023|access-date=5 April 2023|work=Formula 1|archive-date=2 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230402100445/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-haas-protest-results-of-2023-australian-grand-prix.2VIj7H2Oe3V3AAPGESJOdd.html|url-status=live}}</ref> At the [[2023 Canadian Grand Prix|Canadian Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg qualified in second in a wet qualifying session in which he benefited when [[McLaren]]'s [[Oscar Piastri]] crashed at the early part of the qualifying session which brought out the red flag. The rest of the grid were not able to improve on their qualifying as the rain got heavier when the session restarted. However, Hülkenberg was handed a three-place grid penalty for a red flag infraction and started the race in fifth position.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2023 Canadian Grand Prix qualifying report and highlights: Verstappen charges to pole for Canadian GP as Hulkenberg grabs surprise P2 before being hit with grid drop {{!}} Formula 1® |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.verstappen-charges-to-pole-for-canadian-gp-as-hulkenberg-grabs-surprise-p2.snbFfMsKnoRZy2J7Rjxjr.html |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en |archive-date=18 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230618135329/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.verstappen-charges-to-pole-for-canadian-gp-as-hulkenberg-grabs-surprise-p2.snbFfMsKnoRZy2J7Rjxjr.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Boxall-Legge |first=Jake |date=18 June 2023 |title=Hulkenberg handed three-place F1 grid penalty for Canadian GP red flag infraction |url=https://autosport.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-handed-three-place-grid-penalty-for-f1-canadian-gp-red-flag-infraction/10484432/ |access-date=18 June 2023 |website=autosport.motorsport.com |language=en |archive-date=18 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230618075329/https://autosport.motorsport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-handed-three-place-grid-penalty-for-f1-canadian-gp-red-flag-infraction/10484432/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
In the race, excessive tyre wear and the unfortunate timing of a safety car meant he eventually finished 15th. At the [[2023 Austrian Grand Prix|Austrian Grand Prix]], Hülkenberg qualified an impressive 4th for the Sprint in wet conditions. The Sprint, also run in wet conditions, saw him take 2nd place on the first lap, but he eventually finished 6th, securing 3 crucial points for the struggling Haas team, to take them into 7th in the Constructor's standings.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-knew-he-had-no-chance-of-f1-podium-in-austrian-gp-sprint-race/10490223/ | title=Hulkenberg knew he had no chance of F1 podium in Austrian GP sprint race | date=July 2023 }}</ref> For the main grand prix, Hülkenberg qualified an impressive 8th, but had to retire early in the race with an engine issue.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.haasf1team.com/news/austrian-grand-prix-race-recap-3 | title=Austrian Grand Prix: Race Recap &#124; Haas F1 Team }}</ref> By the summer break, Hülkenberg had qualified in the top 10 an impressive 6 times, compared to his teammate's singular appearance. However, the Haas team's season-long struggle with tyre wear meant that neither he nor Haas had managed to add to their points tally since Austria as Haas slipped to 8th in the championship.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.haasf1team.com/news/belgian-grand-prix-race-recap-5 | title=Belgian Grand Prix: Race Recap &#124; Haas F1 Team }}</ref>

Hülkenberg finished 16th in the championship with 9 points to teammate Magnussen's 3. Despite the low points tally he was praised for his qualifying performances, during the season he qualified in the top ten 11 times (3 times in sprint qualifying and 8 times in normal qualifying) even though he had been out of a full time seat for three seasons.

====2024====
[[File:Nico Hulkenberg 2024 Chinese GP.jpg|thumb|left|Hülkenberg at the [[2024 Chinese Grand Prix]]]]
In August 2023, Haas announced that Hülkenberg and Magnussen had been retained for the 2024 season.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Benson |first=Andrew |date=24 August 2023 |title=Haas team to retain Hulkenberg and Magnussen |language=en-GB |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/66600477 |access-date=24 August 2023}}</ref> He earned his first point of the season by finishing 10th at the [[2024 Saudi Arabian Grand Prix]] with the help of strong defending from his teammate.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 March 2024 |title=Hulkenberg in Magnussen vow following Jeddah heroics |url=https://racingnews365.com/hulkenberg-in-magnussen-vow-following-jeddah-heroics |access-date=26 April 2024 |website=RacingNews365 |language=en}}</ref> He continued his good run of form by scoring points in [[2024 Australian Grand Prix|Australia]] and [[2024 Chinese Grand Prix|China]] as well.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Results |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html |access-date=26 April 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref> He finished 6th at the [[2024 Austrian Grand Prix|Austrian Grand Prix]] after holding off the [[Red Bull Racing RB20|Red Bull]] of Sergio Perez at the end of the race. Magnussen finished 8th, marking Haas's best result of the season and moving the team up to 7th place in the standings.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Austrian Grand Prix: Race Recap {{!}} Haas F1 Team |url=https://www.haasf1team.com/news/austrian-grand-prix-race-recap-4 |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=www.haasf1team.com |language=en}}</ref> He repeated this result at the [[2024 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]], qualifying 6th and finishing in that position.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hulkenberg confident Haas can fight to be fifth-fastest team |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/hulkenberg-confident-haas-can-fight-to-be-fifth-fastest-team-after-very.6JgP0qRxDMdQ3R2Zct0Cxz |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref> He again qualified 6th at the [[2024 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore Grand Prix]], finishing 9th after a defensive drive.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hulkenberg reflects on defensive Singapore drive |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/i-dont-care-if-its-a-red-bull-hulkenberg-reflects-on-defensive-singapore.1UHD5bHBYwEJLSm48iTxzj |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref> He finished 8th at the [[2024 United States Grand Prix|United States Grand Prix]], and took 9th at the [[2024 Mexico City Grand Prix|Mexico City Grand Prix]] as Magnussen finished 7th.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Magnussen overjoyed with 'perfect day' for Haas in Mexico |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/im-finding-my-rhythm-again-magnussen-overjoyed-with-perfect-day-in-mexico.1kwZikFr2XZLm42FWOpnNs |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref> Hülkenberg was disqualified from a rainy [[2024 São Paulo Grand Prix|São Paulo Grand Prix]] for receiving assistance from marshals to rejoin the race after being stranded, becoming the first driver to receive a black flag since the [[2007 Canadian Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitchell |first=Rory |date=5 November 2024 |title=The last 10 drivers to receive a black flag after Hulkenberg's Sao Paulo GP disqualification |url=https://www.f1oversteer.com/news/the-last-10-drivers-to-receive-a-black-flag-after-nico-hulkenbergs-sao-paulo-grand-prix-disqualification/ |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=F1 Oversteer |language=en-GB}}</ref> After finishing 8th in [[2024 Las Vegas Grand Prix|Las Vegas]] and failing to finish in [[2024 Qatar Grand Prix|Qatar]], Hülkenberg qualified 4th at the season-ending [[2024 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi Grand Prix]], in front of the Red Bull of champion [[Max Verstappen]]. However, he started 7th after being given a three-place grid penalty for overtaking in the pit-road exit.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hulkenberg hit with grid penalty for Abu Dhabi GP |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-hulkenberg-hit-with-three-place-grid-penalty-for-abu-dhabi-gp.7f9H9fwHYeWQ7VCUwLYVo0 |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref> He finished 8th in the race, but fell to 11th place in the standings after [[Pierre Gasly]] finished in 7th.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2024 Driver Standings |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results/2024/drivers |access-date=14 December 2024 |website=Formula 1® - The Official F1® Website |language=en}}</ref>

=== Return to Sauber / Audi (2025–present) ===
==== 2025: Maiden podium with Sauber ====
[[File:2025 Japan GP - Sauber - Nico Hulkenberg - FP1.jpg|thumb|right|Hülkenberg (pictured at the {{F1GP|2025|Japanese}}) returned to [[Sauber]] in {{F1|2025}}, ahead of their acquisition by [[Audi in Formula One|Audi]].|alt=Hülkenberg driving the Kick Sauber C45 at the 2025 Japanese Grand Prix]]
Hülkenberg left [[Haas F1 Team|Haas]] for his {{F1|2025}} campaign, with [[Sauber]] signing him in preparation for [[Audi]]'s takeover of the team from {{F1|2026}} and beyond.<ref name="contract">{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-to-join-sauber-in-2025-ahead-of-audi-f1-entry/10603345/ |title=Hulkenberg to join Sauber in 2025 ahead of Audi F1 entry |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=26 April 2024 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=12 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207113545/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-to-join-sauber-in-2025-ahead-of-audi-f1-entry/10603345/ |archive-date=7 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> He is partnered by reigning [[2024 Formula 2 Championship|FIA Formula 2]] champion [[Gabriel Bortoleto]]; the two replaced [[Valtteri Bottas]] and [[Zhou Guanyu]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/ckg7d47l2wzo |title=Sauber sign rookie Bortoleto for 2025 season |last=Benson |first=Andrew |date=6 November 2024 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241119034432/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/ckg7d47l2wzo |archive-date=19 November 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> In a wet season-opening {{F1GP|2025|Australian}}, Hülkenberg finished seventh to claim six points—more than Sauber had achieved in the entirety of {{F1|2024}}.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sport/formula-one/article/lando-norris-australian-grand-prix-xs552drkd |url-access=subscription |title=Lando Norris rises above chaos to win wet Australian Grand Prix |last=Hudson |first=Molly |date=16 March 2025 |work=[[The Times]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=0140-0460 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403223513/https://www.thetimes.com/sport/formula-one/article/lando-norris-australian-grand-prix-xs552drkd |archive-date=3 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> After seven Grands Prix without points,{{efn|The [[2025 Chinese Grand Prix|Chinese]], [[2025 Japanese Grand Prix|Japanese]], [[2025 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]], [[2025 Saudi Arabian Grand Prix|Saudi Arabian]], [[2025 Miami Grand Prix|Miami]], [[2025 Emilia Romagna Grand Prix|Emilia Romagna]], and [[2025 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]] Grands Prix.}} including a disqualification in [[2025 Bahrain Grand Prix|Bahrain]] for excessive [[skid block]] wear,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-disqualified-from-bahrain-gp-for-skid-wear-infringement/10713143/ |title=Hülkenberg disqualified from Bahrain GP for skid wear infringement |last=Harrington |first=Alex |date=13 April 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250413193308/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-disqualified-from-bahrain-gp-for-skid-wear-infringement/10713143/ |archive-date=13 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> upgrades for the {{F1GP|2025|Spanish}} saw Sauber emerge as a [[Glossary of motorsport terms#M|midfield]] challenger, with Hülkenberg qualifying sixteenth and finishing fifth after a late [[safety car]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.racecar-engineering.com/articles/f1/updates-that-helped-sauber-log-its-best-result-in-three-years/ |title=Updates that Helped Sauber Log its Best Result in Three Years |last=Lloyd |first=Daniel |date=5 June 2025 |magazine=Racecar Engineering |publisher=The Chelsea Magazine Company |access-date=12 July 2025 |issn=2754-7647 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250618185739/https://www.racecar-engineering.com/articles/f1/updates-that-helped-sauber-log-its-best-result-in-three-years/ |archive-date=18 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/saubers-pinch-yourself-moment-after-nico-hulkenberg-beats-lewis-hamiltons-ferrari/10729319/ |title=Sauber's "pinch yourself moment" after Hülkenberg beats Hamilton's Ferrari |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=3 June 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=12 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250604020055/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/saubers-pinch-yourself-moment-after-nico-hulkenberg-beats-lewis-hamiltons-ferrari/10729319/ |archive-date=4 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He then claimed eighth at the {{F1GP|2025|Canadian}}, having started eleventh,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/hulkenberg-says-upgrades-are-paying-dividends-for-kick-sauber-as-he-scores.1Z2JjsjUkCK7UkEKxFVkB4 |title=Hülkenberg says upgrades are 'paying dividends' for Kick Sauber as he scores for second race in a row |date=17 June 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250713010521/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/hulkenberg-says-upgrades-are-paying-dividends-for-kick-sauber-as-he-scores.1Z2JjsjUkCK7UkEKxFVkB4 |archive-date=13 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and ninth in [[2025 Austrian Grand Prix|Austria]]—behind teammate Bortoleto—after qualifying last on the [[Glossary of motorsport terms#G|grid]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/2025-austrian-grand-prix-f1-driver-rankings-ben-anderson/ |title=Ben Anderson's 2025 Austrian Grand Prix F1 driver rankings |last=Anderson |first=Ben |date=30 June 2025 |website=The Race |publisher=The Race Media |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250630194041/https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/2025-austrian-grand-prix-f1-driver-rankings-ben-anderson/ |archive-date=30 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

At the rain-affected {{F1GP|2025|British}}, Hülkenberg finished third to secure his maiden podium finish in Formula One after starting nineteenth, ending his [[List of Formula One driver records#Most races before scoring a podium finish|record 239-race streak]] without a top-three finish;<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/c78n1g55w1vo |title=Hulkenberg's first podium 'emotional rollercoaster' |last=Lorraine |first=McKenna |date=6 July 2025 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250708053732/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/articles/c78n1g55w1vo |archive-date=8 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> on a "perfectly-timed" strategy,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-was-in-denial-ahead-of-emotional-f1-british-gp-podium/10739982/ |title=Hülkenberg was "in denial" ahead of emotional F1 British GP podium |last=Mee |first=Lydia |date=6 July 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250706173251/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/hulkenberg-was-in-denial-ahead-of-emotional-f1-british-gp-podium/10739982/ |archive-date=6 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> he held off the late advances of [[Lewis Hamilton]] to claim Sauber's first podium in [[2012 Japanese Grand Prix|13 years]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/gabriel-bortoletos-heartfelt-radio-message-to-nico-hulkenberg-defines-sportsmanship/10740111/ |title=Gabriel Bortoleto's heartfelt radio message to Nico Hülkenberg defines sportsmanship |last=Harrington |first=Alex |date=7 July 2025 |website=[[Motorsport.com]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250707124313/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/gabriel-bortoletos-heartfelt-radio-message-to-nico-hulkenberg-defines-sportsmanship/10740111/ |archive-date=7 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> His performance was widely acclaimed by drivers and critics,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/why-the-entire-f1-pack-is-praising-hulkenbergs-maiden-podium/10740061/ |title=Why the entire F1 pack is praising Hülkenberg's maiden podium |last=Mann-Bryans |first=Mark |date=7 July 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250707061828/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/why-the-entire-f1-pack-is-praising-hulkenbergs-maiden-podium/10740061/ |archive-date=7 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> with several remarking that his first podium was "overdue".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6475922/2025/07/06/f1-british-gp-nico-hulkenberg-podium-silverstone/ |title=After 239 races, Nico Hülkenberg scores 'the most overdue podium in F1 history' |last=Smith |first=Luke |date=6 July 2025 |work=[[The Athletic]] |publisher=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=1553-8095 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250707082433/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/6475922/2025/07/06/f1-british-gp-nico-hulkenberg-podium-silverstone/ |archive-date=7 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/racing/f1/story/_/id/45689020/i-never-doubted-f1-celebrates-nico-hulkenberg-long-overdue-first-podium |title='I never doubted him': F1 toasts Hülkenberg's first podium |last=Saunders |first=Nate |date=7 July 2025 |website=[[ESPN]] |publisher=[[ESPN Inc.]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250709134800/https://www.espn.co.uk/racing/f1/story/_/id/45689020/i-never-doubted-f1-celebrates-nico-hulkenberg-long-overdue-first-podium |archive-date=9 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/a-long-time-injustice-is-rectified-british-gp-takeaways/ |url-access=registration |title=A long-time injustice is rectified – British GP takeaways |last=Elizalde |first=Pablo |date=7 July 2025 |magazine=[[Motor Sport (magazine)|Motor Sport]] |access-date=13 July 2025 |issn=0027-2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250713030841/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/a-long-time-injustice-is-rectified-british-gp-takeaways/ |archive-date=13 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> He dropped outside the points again with twelfth in [[2025 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]] and thirteenth in [[2025 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.autoweek.com/racing/formula-1/a65530350/f1-belgian-gp-notebook-hamilton-hulkenberg-alonso/ |title=F1 Belgian GP Notebook: Forgettable Weekends for Hamilton, Hulkenberg, Alonso |last=Horton |first=Phillip |date=28 July 2025 |magazine=[[Autoweek]] |publisher=[[Hearst Communications]] |access-date=5 August 2025 |issn=0192-9674 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250805110153/https://www.autoweek.com/racing/formula-1/a65530350/f1-belgian-gp-notebook-hamilton-hulkenberg-alonso/ |archive-date=5 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/nico-hulkenberg-questions-false-start-penalty-after-tough-hungarian-gp-i-didnt-do-anything-wrong/10748194/ |title=Nico Hülkenberg questions false start penalty after "tough" Hungarian GP: "I didn't do anything wrong" |last=Harrington |first=Alex |date=3 August 2025 |website=[[Motorsport.com]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=5 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250805110415/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/nico-hulkenberg-questions-false-start-penalty-after-tough-hungarian-gp-i-didnt-do-anything-wrong/10748194/ |archive-date=5 August 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== 2026: Audi ====
[[File:2026 Chinese GP - Audi - Nico Hulkenberg - Qualifying.jpg|left|thumb|250x250px|Hülkenberg at the [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix]] driving the [[Audi R26]]]]
Hülkenberg is contracted to retain his place alongside Bortoleto at the re-branded [[Audi in Formula One|Audi]] in {{F1|2026}}.<ref name="contract"/>

==Other racing==
===Sportscar racing===
{{multiple image|total_width=400|image1=Les vainqueurs dans la ligne droite des Hunaudières. (18641735290).jpg|width1=2244|height1=1496|image2=Le Mans 2015 (18819881892) CROP.jpg|width2=751|height2=609|align = left|footer=Left photo: Hülkenberg's No. 19 [[Porsche 919 Hybrid]], which won the [[2015 24 Hours of Le Mans]].<br/>Right photo: Hülkenberg (right) on the podium alongside his teammates [[Nick Tandy]] and [[Earl Bamber]]|direction=horizontal}}
It was confirmed in November 2014 that Hülkenberg would compete in the [[2015 24 Hours of Le Mans]] in a third [[factory-backed]] [[Porsche 919 Hybrid]]. He also contested the [[2015 6 Hours of Spa-Francorchamps|Spa-Francorchamps]] round of the [[2015 FIA World Endurance Championship season|World Endurance Championship]] as preparation.<ref>{{cite news|title=Force India F1 driver Hulkenberg joins Porsche for Le Mans 24 Hours|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/116960|work=[[Autosport]]|date=27 November 2014|access-date=27 November 2014|archive-date=11 September 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160911035427/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/116960|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 14 June 2015, he won the 24 Hours of Le Mans race, driving alongside Britain's [[Nick Tandy]] and [[Earl Bamber]] of New Zealand. They completed 395 laps, one lap ahead of the car of their Porsche teammates [[Mark Webber (racing driver)|Mark Webber]], [[Brendon Hartley]] and [[Timo Bernhard]], who took second place. This win was Porsche's first overall victory in the event since the [[1998 24 Hours of Le Mans]].<ref name=as2015LM>{{cite news |title= Le Mans 24 Hours: Porsche wins with Hülkenberg, Tandy and Bamber |author= Freeman, Glenn |url= http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/119499 |date= 14 June 2015 |work= Autosport |access-date= 14 June 2015 |archive-date= 3 March 2016 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160303174224/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/119499 |url-status= live }}</ref> Hülkenberg's triumph made him the first active Formula One driver to win at Le Mans since [[Johnny Herbert]] and [[Bertrand Gachot]], who performed the same feat in [[1991 24 Hours of Le Mans|1991]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Hulkenberg wins Le Mans in Porsche one-two|url=https://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/news/hulkenberg-leads-le-mans-porsche-075923301--f1.html|website=Eurosport Yahoo!|access-date=15 June 2015|date=14 June 2015|archive-date=1 July 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230701110701/https://consent.yahoo.com/v2/collectConsent?sessionId=2_cc-session_2bf5bb9a-cde2-4158-b18c-aaf3c14d1bd6|url-status=live}}</ref>

===IndyCar===
On 25 October 2021, Hülkenberg took part in a private IndyCar test at [[Barber Motorsports Park]], driving the No. 7 [[Arrow McLaren SP]] (AMSP) car, with AMSP team principal Taylor Kiel stating in a report that Hülkenberg was in consideration for a third part-time entry in 2022.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Foster |first1=Michelle |title=Hulkenberg to test a McLaren IndyCar at Barber |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/nico-hulkenbergtest-mclaren-indycar/ |website=Planet F1 |date=20 October 2021 |access-date=20 October 2021 |archive-date=20 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020123440/https://www.planetf1.com/news/nico-hulkenbergtest-mclaren-indycar/ |url-status=live }}</ref> After completing over 100 laps, he set a best time of 77.454 seconds in his first test, roughly a second off of the fastest time, which was set by [[2021 Indy Lights]] runner-up [[David Malukas]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=25 October 2021|title=Malukas tops five-car IndyCar test at Barber|url=https://racer.com/2021/10/25/malukas-tops-five-car-indycar-test-at-barber/|access-date=26 October 2021|website=RACER|language=en-US|archive-date=30 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220930222503/https://racer.com/2021/10/25/malukas-tops-five-car-indycar-test-at-barber/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=26 October 2021|title=Hulkenberg revels in first IndyCar test with AMSP|url=https://racer.com/2021/10/25/hulkenberg-revels-in-first-indycar-test-with-amsp/|access-date=26 October 2021|website=RACER|language=en-US|archive-date=30 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220930222505/https://racer.com/2021/10/25/hulkenberg-revels-in-first-indycar-test-with-amsp/|url-status=live}}</ref> Hülkenberg ultimately declined an opportunity to pursue a career in IndyCar with McLaren, stating that he was unwilling to race on ovals and that he found the [[Dallara DW12]] to be significantly harder to drive physically than the Formula One cars he was used to.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Media |first1=Global motorsports |title=Former F1 Racer Nico Hulkenberg Says No Thanks to 'Difficult to Drive' IndyCar Monsters |url=https://www.autoweek.com/racing/indycar/a38202570/hulkenberg-no-thanks-indycar-monsters/ |website=Autoweek |date=9 November 2021 |access-date=10 November 2021 |archive-date=9 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211109231305/https://www.autoweek.com/racing/indycar/a38202570/hulkenberg-no-thanks-indycar-monsters/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Although refusing to race IndyCars on ovals, Hülkenberg does actually have race experience on a superspeedway. As part of his [[2006 German Formula Three Championship]] campaign for team [[Josef Kaufmann Racing]], he raced in the ADAC East Side 100, an oval race on the [[Eurospeedway Lausitz]].

== Other ventures ==
Hülkenberg has entered his own team in the [[eSkootr Championship]], named "27X by Nico Hülkenberg".<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 May 2022 |title=Nico Hulkenberg to enter a team in the new eSkootr Championship {{!}} eSkootrChampionship™ |url=https://official.esc.live/latest/nico-hulkenberg-to-enter-a-team-in-the-new-eskootr-championship |access-date=15 May 2022 |website=official.esc.live |archive-date=17 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220517052423/https://official.esc.live/latest/nico-hulkenberg-to-enter-a-team-in-the-new-eskootr-championship |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=7 May 2022 |title=Nico Hulkenberg to enter a team in the new eSkootr Championship |url=https://thephoenixnewspaper.com/nico-hulkenberg-to-enter-a-team-in-the-new-eskootr-championship |access-date=15 May 2022 |website=The Phoenix Newspaper |language=en-gb |archive-date=7 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220507085522/https://thephoenixnewspaper.com/nico-hulkenberg-to-enter-a-team-in-the-new-eskootr-championship |url-status=live }}</ref>

